WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 77 for June 19th, 2026.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.740
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today. Let's dive in.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:16.120
Something wild just happened with health questions and chat GPT. Open AI announced that

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:22.720
it's new GPT 5.5 instant model now performs at the same level as its top thinking models.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:28.240
When people ask about medical topics, think of it like the difference between a quick voice assistant

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.840
that gives short answers and a careful friend who listens, asks follow-up questions and explains

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:41.540
things clearly. GPT 5.5 instant was trained and tested by physicians, so it better recognizes

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:47.940
when something might need urgent care. It asks for helpful context and explains uncertainty instead

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:54.260
of guessing. This matters because more than 230 million people every week turned to chat GPT

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:59.160
with health questions. For students or anyone without easy access to a doctor,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.960
having a free tool that gives clear, safer sounding answers could change how people get initial

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:10.380
information. It also shows that speed and quality do not always have to trade off.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.960
The same model that answers almost instantly can now handle serious topics responsibly for you,

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:22.960
personally, this means the free chat GPT you might already use on your phone just became

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:28.980
more reliable for everyday questions about feeling sick, nutrition, or mental health. It will not

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.200
replace real medical care, but it can help you figure out whether you should talk to a parent or doctor

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:41.520
sooner. Right now, you can open the free chat GPT app or website and try asking a specific question

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.420
like this. Say you have had a headache for two days after staring at screens and ask what you should

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:53.900
consider. Notice how it asks for more details and explains when it is unsure. That is the new

00:01:53.900 --> 00:02:00.500
capability in action. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive, where we look under the

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:06.480
hood. You know how when you are texting a friend and your phone suggests the next word, it sometimes

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.480
gets the tone right because it has learned from millions of your past messages. Now imagine that same

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.760
auto-climate system also learned when to pause and say, I am not sure can you tell me more instead

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:26.040
of just guessing. That is basically what is happening inside GPT 5.5 instant when it answers health

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:32.300
questions. The model was shown thousands of real doctor-written answers and doctor-reviewed conversations.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:38.900
It learned patterns like when someone mentions chest pain and shortness of breath. First check if this

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:45.040
sounds urgent. At the same time, it was trained to notice its own uncertainty. For example,

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:51.220
when symptoms could mean many different things, it asks for more context rather than make something up.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:58.420
The result is an AI that does not just spit out the most likely next sentence. It weighs safety,

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.820
clarity and honesty before answering. So next time someone says AI just predicts the next word,

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:10.620
you can tell them it is more like a very careful auto-coupleat that was taught when to stop and ask

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:16.660
questions instead. And that is basically how this careful health answering works. Not so scary, right?

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:23.800
Now let us look at some cool stuff you can try yourself. A new survey from Match found that 47%

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:30.660
of US singles feel negative about AI being used in dating. At the same time, many people are open to

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.180
AI helping write better profile bios or suggesting conversation starters.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:41.340
The interesting part is the split. People do not like the idea of AI secretly picking matches

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:47.060
for them, but they are fine with it as a writing helper. This connects directly to how you might

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:52.940
already use AI for school essays or social media captions. The same tool feels different when it

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:58.480
touches something as personal as dating. If you have a dating app account or are just curious,

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:05.640
try this experiment right now. Open any profile you have written and ask chat GPT to help you rewrite

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:11.920
this bio to sound more like me, but clear. See what it changes and what still feels like your own voice.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.480
That small experiment shows exactly where people draw the line with AI help.

